   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the public authorities law, in relation to providing that subcontractors on New York city school construction projects who file mechanics' liens shall not be entitled to payment protection through the authority",
    "basePrintNo": "A10147",
    "basePrintNoStr": "A10147-2009",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §1735, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "BILL NUMBER:A10147\n\nTITLE OF BILL:  An act to amend the public authorities law, in\nrelation to providing that subcontractors on New York city school\nconstruction projects who file mechanics' liens shall not be entitled\nto payment protection through the authority\n\nPURPOSE:  To double withholding of payments to contractors on New York\nCity School Construction Authority construction contracts.\n\nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:  Amends Section subparagraph 6 of Section 1735\nof Public Authorities Law to clarify situations relative to\ncontractors failure to pay certain subcontractors on New York City\nSchool Construction Authority Work in that the special payment\nprotections are intended to be used in lieu of a lien filing and not\nin addition to such filing.\n\nJUSTIFICATION:  When the New York City School Construction Authority\nwas created, special payment protections were granted to the\nmechanical and electrical subcontractors in order that the SCA would\nbe free of the requirements of the Wicks Law. One of those protections\nwas to allow those subcontractors to seek help from the SCA where, it\nwas alleged that the general contractor had failed to pay the\nsubcontractor timely. That help would include the withholding of money\ndue and owing the general contractor in order that the subcontractor\nmay be paid directly or for the money to be placed in escrow pending\nresolution of a dispute. In practice, however, some subcontractors\nhave sought such assistance from the SCA in addition to the filing to\nmechanic's liens. Those liens carry with them the obligation on the\npart of the owner to withhold 1 1/2 times the amount of lien from\npayment to the contractor in addition to the special withholding\ncalled for in Section 1735. This double withholding places a\ntremendous financial burden on the contractor, partially where the\nmatter is in dispute, and would, in fact, cause cash flow problems to\nother subcontractors on the project. This legislation would correct\nhis matter and require the subcontractor to choose between the lien\nand the special protections granted in 1735. As these protections are\nsuperior to the filing of a lien, it is likely that the choice will be\nfor the protections granted herein.\n\nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY: S2725 (2008)\n\nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS: None\n\nEFFECTIVE DATE: Immediately.\n\n",
